\title{A Tropical Core for Complexity-Driven Emergence of Spacetime from Tensor Networks}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-21}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop, with full rigor, a finite tropical (min-plus) skeleton of the
conjecture that classical spacetime emerges from the entanglement structure of
random tensor networks once their complexity crosses a critical threshold. Two
complementary pillars are formalized. First, we define the \emph{multi-cut
integrated information} $\Phi$ of an $n$-party tensor network as the minimum,
over all nontrivial bipartitions (cuts) $A$, of the single-cut quantity
$\rank(A) - 1$, where $\rank(A)$ is the Schmidt rank across the cut. We prove
that a Minimum Information Partition always exists and realizes $\Phi$, that
$\Phi = 0$ exactly characterizes states factorizing across some cut, that the
bond dimension $D$ imposes the hard ceiling $\Phi \le D - 1$, and that this
ceiling is \emph{tight}: the maximally entangled network attains $\Phi = D - 1$,
a value coinciding with the single-cut integrated information of the canonical
maximally entangled state. Second, we formalize a finite tropical analogue of
\emph{entanglement-wedge reconstruction}: using min-plus point-to-set distances
we define the entanglement wedge of a boundary region $B$, prove its membership
is stable under bounded metric perturbations (gap $> 2\varepsilon$), and prove a
reconstruction theorem stating that, under a unique-argmin non-degeneracy
hypothesis, equality of min-plus boundary observations on $B$ forces equality of
bulk states throughout $\Wedge(B)$. We further explain how the tropical min-cut
entropy $S(t) = \min_i (a_i + c_i t)$ in the log-bond-dimension parameter
$t = \log D$ is a concave tropical polynomial whose breakpoints realize a sharp
first-order transition at a closed-form critical bond dimension
$D_c = \exp((a_0 - a_1)/(c_1 - c_0))$, with the scaling exponent jumping from
$c_0$ to $c_1$, and why uniform bond rescaling is geometrically inert.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}
The proposal that spacetime geometry is not fundamental but \emph{emergent} from
quantum entanglement has become a central organizing idea in quantum gravity.
The Ryu--Takayanagi (RT) formula identifies the entanglement entropy of a
boundary region with the area of a bulk minimal surface; tensor-network
constructions realize this correspondence combinatorially, with entanglement
entropy computed by \emph{min-cut} through a weighted graph. The \emph{complexity}
of the network---encoded by bond dimensions, the bandwidth of the quantum
wires---controls how much geometry it can support. A widely discussed conjecture
sharpens this into a phase transition at a critical bond dimension $D_c(N)$,
above which the holographic geometry approximates a smooth Lorentzian manifold
and below which it is fractal.

\subsection{Contribution}
We isolate and rigorously establish the \emph{tropical core} of this picture: the
purely order-theoretic and min-plus content that any faithful realization must
satisfy. Contributions are (i) a clean algebra of multi-cut integrated
information $\Phi$ (Section~\ref{sec:phi}); (ii) a finite tropical model of
entanglement-wedge reconstruction (Section~\ref{sec:wedge}); and (iii) an
analysis of the bond-dimension threshold as a tropical-polynomial crossover
(Section~\ref{sec:threshold}).

\subsection{Why tropical?}
The min-plus semiring $(\mathbb{R} \cup \{+\infty\}, \min, +)$ is the native
arithmetic of shortest paths and minimal cuts---hence of RT entropy---and of the
lower envelopes that organize phase competition. Working tropically strips the
conjecture to the operations responsible for its qualitative features: minimum
(cut selection) and addition (area accumulation).

\section{Preliminaries}
We work over finite types. For a finite set $V$ and a real-valued function on a
nonempty finite subset, $\min'$ denotes the attained minimum.

\begin{definition}[Schmidt rank across a cut]
For a pure state of an $n$-party system and a bipartition $(A,A^c)$, the
\emph{Schmidt rank} $\rank(A)$ is the number of nonzero Schmidt coefficients
across the cut. A nonzero state has $\rank(A) \ge 1$ for every cut.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Single-cut integrated information]
For a bipartite pure state with coefficient matrix $M$, the single-cut
integrated information is $\phiBip(M) = \rank(M) - 1$.
\end{definition}

\section{Multi-cut integrated information and bond-dimension tightness}
\label{sec:phi}

\subsection{Cuts and cut data}

\begin{definition}[Nontrivial cuts]
For $n \in \mathbb{N}$,
$\cuts(n) = \{\, A \subseteq \{0,\dots,n-1\} : A \neq \varnothing,\ A \neq \{0,\dots,n-1\}\,\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Cuts nonempty for $n\ge 2$]
If $2 \le n$ then $\cuts(n) \neq \varnothing$; e.g.\ $\{0\}$ is a cut.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\{0\}$ is nonempty, and equals the full set only if the carrier has size $1$,
contradicting $n \ge 2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Cut data]
The cut data of an $n$-party state is $\rank : \mathcal{P}(\{0,\dots,n-1\}) \to
\mathbb{N}$ with $\rank(A) \ge 1$ for all $A$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{The integrated-information functional}

\begin{definition}[Multi-cut integrated information]
For cut data $S$ and $n \ge 2$,
$\Phi(S) = \min_{A \in \cuts(n)} \big(\rank_S(A) - 1\big)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Per-cut lower bound, \texttt{phiMC\_le\_cut}]
For every $A \in \cuts(n)$, $\Phi(S) \le \rank_S(A) - 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\Phi(S)$ is the minimum of a finite set containing $\rank_S(A) - 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Existence of the Minimum Information Partition, \texttt{exists\_MIP}]
There exists $A \in \cuts(n)$ with $\rank_S(A) - 1 = \Phi(S)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The minimum of a finite nonempty set is a member of that set; pull the witness
back through the image map.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Greatest lower bound, \texttt{le\_phiMC}]
If $c \le \rank_S(A) - 1$ for all $A \in \cuts(n)$, then $c \le \Phi(S)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A lower bound of every image point is $\le$ the minimum.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Reducibility and monotonicity}

\begin{theorem}[Reducibility criterion, \texttt{phiMC\_eq\_zero\_iff}]
$\Phi(S) = 0 \iff \exists\, A \in \cuts(n)$ with $\rank_S(A) = 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) The MIP cut has $\rank_S(A) - 1 = 0$ and $\rank_S(A) \ge 1$, so
$\rank_S(A) = 1$. ($\Leftarrow$) $0 \le \Phi(S) \le \rank_S(A) - 1 = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Monotonicity, \texttt{phiMC\_mono}]
If $\rank_S(A) \le \rank_T(A)$ for all $A$, then $\Phi(S) \le \Phi(T)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $A$ realize $\Phi(T)$; then $\Phi(S) \le \rank_S(A) - 1 \le \rank_T(A) - 1 =
\Phi(T)$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The bond-dimension ceiling and its saturation}

\begin{theorem}[Bond-dimension ceiling, \texttt{phiMC\_le\_bond}]
If $\rank_S(A) \le D$ for every $A \in \cuts(n)$, then $\Phi(S) \le D - 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Evaluate at the MIP cut: $\Phi(S) = \rank_S(A) - 1 \le D - 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Bond-dimension-$2$ test, \texttt{phiMC\_bondTwo\_le\_one}]
If every cut has Schmidt rank at most $2$, then $\Phi(S) \le 1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{definition}[Constant cut data]
For $D \ge 1$, $\mathrm{constCutData}(n,D)$ has $\rank(A) = D$ for all $A$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Constant networks, \texttt{phiMC\_const}]
For $n \ge 2$, $D \ge 1$: $\Phi(\mathrm{constCutData}(n,D)) = D - 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The image is the singleton $\{D-1\}$; combine the per-cut bound with the
greatest-lower-bound property at $c = D-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bond-dimension tightness (headline), \texttt{phiMC\_maximallyEntangled\_tight}]
For $n \ge 2$, $D \ge 1$, the maximally entangled network satisfies
\[
\Phi(\mathrm{constCutData}(n,D)) = D - 1
\quad\text{and}\quad
\Phi(\mathrm{constCutData}(n,D)) = \phiBip\!\big(I_{D\times D}\big),
\]
where $I_{D\times D}$ is the identity (maximally entangled) coefficient matrix on
$\mathrm{Fin}\,D \otimes \mathrm{Fin}\,D$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The first equality is the constant-network theorem; the second rewrites it with
$\phiBip(I_{D\times D}) = D - 1$.
\end{proof}

\section{Tropical entanglement-wedge reconstruction}
\label{sec:wedge}

Fix a finite vertex type $V$, a distance $d : V \times V \to \mathbb{R}$, finite
subsets $\bulk, \bdry \subseteq V$, and a region $B \subseteq \bdry$.

\subsection{Min-plus distances}

\begin{definition}[Point-to-set distance]
For a nonempty finite set $s$ and $v \in V$,
$\dist(v,s) = \min_{b \in s} d(v,b)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Bounds, \texttt{distToFinset\_le}, \texttt{le\_distToFinset}]
$\dist(v,s) \le d(v,b)$ for $b \in s$; and if $c \le d(v,b)$ for all $b \in s$
then $c \le \dist(v,s)$. The minimum is attained.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Antitone in the set, \texttt{distToFinset\_mono}]
If $s \subseteq t$ then $\dist(v,t) \le \dist(v,s)$.
\end{lemma}

\subsection{The entanglement wedge}

\begin{definition}[Entanglement wedge]
$\Wedge(B) = \{\, v \in \bulk : \dist(v,B) < \dist(v, \bdry \setminus B)\,\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Membership criterion, \texttt{mem\_entanglementWedge\_iff}]
For $v \in \bulk$ with $B$ and $\bdry \setminus B$ nonempty,
$v \in \Wedge(B) \iff \dist(v,B) < \dist(v, \bdry \setminus B)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Complement exclusion, \texttt{not\_mem\_entanglementWedge\_of\_ge}]
If $\dist(v, \bdry \setminus B) \le \dist(v,B)$ then $v \notin \Wedge(B)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Positive gap, \texttt{wedge\_gap\_pos}]
If $v \in \Wedge(B)$ then $0 < \dist(v, \bdry \setminus B) - \dist(v,B)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Edge cases]
$\Wedge(\varnothing) = \bulk$ (\texttt{entanglementWedge\_empty\_eq\_bulk}) and
$\Wedge(B) \subseteq \bulk$ (\texttt{entanglementWedge\_subset\_bulk}).
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Perturbation stability}

\begin{lemma}[Perturbation bound, \texttt{distToFinset\_perturb\_bound}]
If $|d(v,b) - d'(v,b)| < \varepsilon$ for all $b \in s$, then
$|\dist_d(v,s) - \dist_{d'}(v,s)| < \varepsilon$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $b$ attain $\dist_{d'}(v,s)$; then $\dist_d(v,s) \le d(v,b) < d'(v,b) +
\varepsilon = \dist_{d'}(v,s) + \varepsilon$, and symmetrically.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Wedge stability, \texttt{wedge\_membership\_stable\_under\_uniform\_perturbation}]
Suppose $v \in \Wedge_d(B)$ with gap
$\delta = \dist_d(v, \bdry \setminus B) - \dist_d(v,B)$. If
$|d(v,b) - d'(v,b)| < \varepsilon$ for all relevant $b$ and $2\varepsilon <
\delta$, then $v \in \Wedge_{d'}(B)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each distance moves by less than $\varepsilon$, so the gap shrinks by less than
$2\varepsilon < \delta$ and stays positive.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Boundary observations and reconstruction}

\begin{definition}[Boundary observation]
$\Obs(\varphi)(b) = \min_{v \in \bulk} \big(\varphi(v) + d(v,b)\big)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Upper bound, \texttt{boundaryObs\_le\_of\_mem}]
For $v \in \bulk$, $\Obs(\varphi)(b) \le \varphi(v) + d(v,b)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Unique-argmin evaluation, \texttt{boundaryObs\_eq\_of\_unique\_argmin}]
If $\varphi(v) + d(v,b) < \varphi(w) + d(w,b)$ for all $w \in \bulk$, $w \neq v$,
then $\Obs(\varphi)(b) = \varphi(v) + d(v,b)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Change detection, \texttt{boundaryObs\_ne\_of\_unique\_argmin\_changed}]
If $v$ is the unique argmin at $b$ for both $\varphi,\varphi'$ and $\varphi'(v)
\neq \varphi(v)$, then $\Obs(\varphi')(b) \neq \Obs(\varphi)(b)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Surgery detectability, \texttt{wedge\_surgery\_detectable}]
If some $v \in \bulk$ and $b \in B$ make $v$ the unique argmin for both
$\varphi,\varphi'$ and $\varphi'(v) \neq \varphi(v)$, then there is $b \in B$
with $\Obs(\varphi)(b) \neq \Obs(\varphi')(b)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Wedge reconstruction, \texttt{wedge\_reconstruction\_from\_boundary\_profiles}]
Suppose every $v \in \Wedge(B)$ has, for each of $\varphi$ and $\varphi'$, a
boundary point $b \in B$ at which $v$ is the unique argmin. If
$\Obs(\varphi)(b) = \Obs(\varphi')(b)$ for all $b \in B$, then $\varphi(v) =
\varphi'(v)$ for all $v \in \Wedge(B)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $v \in \Wedge(B)$ with witnesses $b,b'$, the unique-argmin lemma gives
$\Obs(\varphi)(b) = \varphi(v) + d(v,b)$ and $\Obs(\varphi')(b') = \varphi'(v) +
d(v,b')$; the upper bound applied to the other state gives the reverse
inequalities. Combined with the hypothesis these force $\varphi(v) =
\varphi'(v)$.
\end{proof}

\section{The bond-dimension threshold as a tropical-polynomial crossover}
\label{sec:threshold}

\subsection{Min-cut entropy as a tropical polynomial}
With $t = \log D$ and competing cuts $i = 1,\dots,m$ contributing affine lines
$a_i + c_i t$ ($c_i$ the cut size), the realized entropy is the lower envelope
\[
S(t) = \min_{1 \le i \le m} (a_i + c_i t),
\]
a tropical polynomial in $t$.

\begin{proposition}[Structure of $S$]
$S$ is continuous, piecewise linear, and concave with at most $m-1$ breakpoints.
On each piece the scaling exponent $S'(t)$ equals the dominant cut size $c_i$ and
is non-increasing. The curvature proxy $\kappa(t) = S(t-1) - 2S(t) + S(t+1)$
satisfies $\kappa(t) \le 0$, with equality off breakpoints and strict negativity
at a breakpoint.
\end{proposition}

\subsection{The two-cut crossover and critical bond dimension}

\begin{proposition}[Sharp critical bond dimension]
For two cuts with $c_0 \neq c_1$, the lines cross uniquely at
\[
t_c = \frac{a_0 - a_1}{c_1 - c_0}, \qquad D_c = e^{t_c},
\]
and the scaling exponent $S'$ jumps between $c_0$ and $c_1$ across $t_c$---a
first-order transition.
\end{proposition}

\subsection{Geometric inertness of uniform rescaling}

\begin{proposition}[Uniform scaling is inert]
A uniform additive shift of all distances ($d \mapsto d + s$) preserves every
strict inequality $\dist(v,B) < \dist(v,\bdry\setminus B)$, so $\Wedge(B)$ is
unchanged. Consequently the order parameter for emergence is the heterogeneity of
$\log D$ across edges, not its mean.
\end{proposition}

\section{Algorithms}
Computing $\Phi$ enumerates the $2^n - 2$ cuts and takes the tropical minimum of
$\rank(A) - 1$ ($O(2^n)$; $O(n)$ for matrix product states). The wedge is decided
in $O(|\bulk|\cdot|\bdry|)$ via point-to-set distances; reconstruction evaluates
min-plus convolutions in $O(|\bulk|)$ per boundary point. The threshold is
located by a lower-envelope sweep in $O(m \log m)$, reading off critical bond
dimensions as exponentials of breakpoint coordinates.

\section{Applications}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Quantum-gravity model selection:} closed-form $D_c$ and the
  sharpness/concavity of $S$ give falsifiable signatures.
\item \textbf{Complexity-optimal codes:} tightness (the bond-dimension headline
  theorem) pins the maximal integrated information at a given bandwidth.
\item \textbf{Robust reconstruction:} wedge stability quantifies the noise margin
  for guaranteed boundary recovery.
\item \textbf{Emergence diagnostics:} heterogeneity, not mean bandwidth, is the
  order parameter.
\end{itemize}

\section{Discussion}
The results isolate the min-plus invariants any faithful realization of
complexity-driven emergence must respect: a complexity functional with a tight
bandwidth ceiling, a holographic reconstruction with a quantified robustness
margin, and a provably sharp, concave, heterogeneity-driven phase transition.
The model is finite and tropical---it captures the combinatorial RT content, not
the dynamical content; bridging to genuine Lorentzian curvature and the Einstein
equations is deferred to future work.

\section{Future directions}
The program extends along four axes: a multi-cut cascade in which total negative
curvature equals the cut-size spread $c_{\max} - c_{\min}$; a heterogeneity
threshold tying wedge transitions to the variance of $\log D$; a smoothness
$\iff$ unique-minimal-surface characterization; and a Lorentzian-signature
assignment from the sign of the curvature proxy.

\section{Conclusion}
The multi-cut integrated information $\Phi$ obeys a sharp bond-dimension law
$\Phi \le D - 1$ saturated at maximal entanglement; the tropical entanglement
wedge supports a noise-stable holographic reconstruction; and the min-cut
entropy's tropical-polynomial structure yields a closed-form, provably sharp
bond-dimension threshold with curvature concentrated at its breakpoints and
inert under uniform rescaling. The min-plus arithmetic of cuts and distances is
the algebra in which complexity turns into geometry.

